<%# THE RULES FORM WRITES EVERY SHAPE BY HAND (rules-own-the-budget spec §4).

    WHAT THIS FORM HAS BEEN, AND WHY THE LAST VERSION HAD TO GO. It was a mode-aware form serving a
    category CAP and a pool rule at once; then a pool-only rule form with an ENVELOPE half for the
    accept flow; then a rule on the category that holds the money — and in all three it could reach
    exactly TWO of the shapes the model allowed. A hand-made rule was always a per-period rate (`basis` rode
    hidden saying so), and a dated bill could only arrive MEASURED, from the suggestion panel, with
    its interval and anchor prefilled. "Rules form needs to be able to set the complex rules too,
    not just the per period catchall" (Henry, 2026-09-04) is the ruling this rewrite encodes.

    THE FIVE CONTROLS: Category · Pays · Type · Amount · When is it needed (with its date and its
    repeats checkbox). Every one of them renders on BOTH paths except the category, which is
    read-only on an edit —
    the page that lists rules groups them BY category, so moving one between categories is a
    re-parenting that list should own rather than a control buried in one rule's form.

    THE SCHEDULE IS EDITABLE ON AN EDIT NOW, and the hint that used to say otherwise ("the schedule
    itself is already set on this rule") is deleted with the branch that showed it. §4 rules a shape
    change legal outright: the claim is COMPUTED, so an allowance made into a dated rule simply has
    the walk re-run from its accrual start under the new shape. Nothing is migrated and nothing is
    stale.

    THE FORM SUBMITS WORDS, NOT COLUMNS. `schedule` is the radio and `repeats` the checkbox;
    `RuleForm` turns them into `basis`, `interval_months` and `anchor_date`, and turns them back for
    this form to render. Nothing here knows that a repeating dated rule is
    `monthly + interval N + anchor`.

    THE REVEALS ARE AN ENHANCEMENT AND NOT A GATE. Each conditional block renders with `hidden` set
    to the state the CURRENT choice implies, and `rule_form_controller.js` toggles it as the radios
    move — but the `<noscript>` rule below forces every one of them visible, so a browser with no
    JavaScript gets the whole form and the server answers whatever it submits. The controller CLEARS
    a field as it hides it (and puts the value back when it returns), which is why `RuleForm` can
    refuse a due date on a per-period rule rather than laundering it away: with JS that refusal is
    unreachable, and without JS the message lands under a control that is on screen. %>
<% rule_form = @rule_form %>
<% budget = rule_form.budget %>
<%# WHETHER THIS FORM IS ASKING WHO OWNS THE RULE. The controller's answer, not a re-derivation:
    "an owner is already in context" is a fact about how the request arrived (a prefill, a persisted
    rule) and cannot be read off the record once the picker's own choice has been assigned to it. %>
<% owner_picker = @owner_picker.present? %>
<% named_owner = !owner_picker && budget.category.present? %>
<% subtitle = "What a category claims each period" %>
<% subtitle = "Pick what this rule is for, and how much it claims each period" if owner_picker %>
<% subtitle = "What #{budget.category.name} claims each period" if named_owner %>
<%# "RULE", NOT "BUDGET" (design review M1). The screen this form is reached from calls the thing a
    RULE in every one of its own words, and the model class keeps its name while the user-facing
    noun is one word everywhere. %>
<%= page_header(title: rule_form.persisted? ? "Edit rule" : "New rule", subtitle: subtitle) %>

<noscript>
  <style>[data-rule-reveal][hidden] { display: block !important; }</style>
</noscript>

<div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
  <%= simple_form_for rule_form,
      as: :budget,
      url: rule_form.persisted? ? budget_path(budget) : budgets_path,
      method: rule_form.persisted? ? :patch : :post,
      html: {
        class: "space-y-6",
        data: {
          controller: "app--budget--rule-form",
          # THE CATEGORY IN FORCE, AS A FACT RATHER THAN AS A FIELD. "Pays" filters by it, and on the
          # EDIT path there is no longer an input carrying it (fix round 1 — M1: the category is not
          # writable there, so the form does not submit it). A Stimulus value says the same thing
          # without putting a writable copy of the owner back on the wire; the picker's own select
          # overrides it while the user is choosing.
          "app--budget--rule-form-category-value": rule_form.category_id
        }
      } do |f| %>
    <%= f.error_notification %>
    <%# ERRORS WITH NOWHERE ELSE TO LAND. simple_form prints an attribute's error under that
        attribute's input, so an error on a field this form does not RENDER produces a 422 whose
        only visible text is "please review the problems below". `:base` is lifted here for that
        reason — an owner-less rule is a fact about the whole record and has no control at all.

        THE OWNER JOINS IT EXACTLY WHEN THE FORM IS NOT ASKING FOR ONE. On the picker path
        `category_must_be_an_expense` lands under the select the user chose in, which is right; on
        the EDIT path the category is a read-only box and a hidden field, so the same message would
        be invisible — and that is the one path the validation exists to close, since `#update` is
        what `BudgetProposal` never sees. %>
    <% lifted = rule_form.errors[:base] + (owner_picker ? [] : rule_form.errors[:category_id]) %>
    <%= f.error_notification message: lifted.to_sentence if lifted.present? %>

    <div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-6">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <%# ── 1. CATEGORY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            `RuleForm#category_options` is this user's EXPENSE categories, which is both halves of
            the scoping: the collection answers WHAT KIND of category may own a rule and the
            association answers WHOSE. A foreign id typed into a submit is refused a second time on
            the way in — `BudgetsController#scoped_owner` looks `category_id` up through
            `current_user.categories` and 404s — so this select is the affordance, not the boundary.

            THE BLANK IS OFFERED because "I have not chosen yet" is a real state on a form that opens
            with nothing chosen, not because a rule may have no owner: `Budget#must_have_a_category`
            refuses the blank on `:base`, which is where this form renders base errors.

            ** THE READ-ONLY BRANCH SUBMITS NOTHING (fix round 1 — M1). ** It used to carry a hidden
            `category_id` so the record "kept the owner it arrived with" — but a persisted rule keeps
            its owner because the ROW has one, and the hidden field's only real effect was to make a
            re-parent a legal PATCH that no control here could ask for. `#update` does not permit the
            key at all now. On the CREATE path that names an owner (a suggestion accept) the hidden
            field stays: there is no row yet, so the form is the only thing that can say who owns it.

            "Pays" still filters by the category in force, which the form element states as a
            Stimulus VALUE — see the `simple_form_for` call above. %>
        <% if owner_picker %>
          <div>
            <%= f.input :category_id,
                as: :select,
                collection: rule_form.category_options,
                label_method: :name,
                value_method: :id,
                include_blank: "Select a category",
                label: "Category",
                hint: "The category this rule claims for each period. A rule is one of the two things " \
                      "that make a category start holding its own money.",
                input_html: {
                  data: {
                    "app--budget--rule-form-target": "category",
                    action: "change->app--budget--rule-form#refresh"
                  }
                } %>
          </div>
        <% elsif named_owner %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Category</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= budget.category.name %>
            </div>
            <%= f.hidden_field :category_id unless rule_form.persisted? %>
          </div>
        <% end %>

        <%# ── 2. PAYS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            WHAT THE RULE PAYS FOR: the whole category (the default, and the shape every rate rule
            has) or ONE of its items. An item-backed rule has a spending lane of its own — the claim
            reads that item's entries to decide whether the bill has been settled this cycle
            (`ClaimCalculator#due_on`) — which is what lets a category carry one catch-all rule and a
            dated bill beside it.

            EVERY ITEM THE USER OWNS IS RENDERED, each stamped with its own category, and the
            controller HIDES the ones that do not belong to the category in force. Filtering in the
            browser rather than re-fetching is what makes the category select feel immediate; and
            because every option is really there, a browser with no JavaScript gets the full list
            and `Budget#item_must_belong_to_category` refuses the wrong one with a legible 422.

            `item_id` IS OWNERSHIP-SCOPED ON THE WAY IN (`BudgetsController#scoped_owners`): a
            stranger's id would write a rule against THEIR spending, so it 404s rather than
            rendering their item's name here. %>
        <div>
          <label class="form-label" for="budget_item_id">Pays</label>
          <%= f.select :item_id,
              options_for_select(
                rule_form.item_options.map { |item| [item.name, item.id, { "data-category-id" => item.category_id }] },
                rule_form.item_id
              ),
              { include_blank: "The whole category" },
              class: "form-select",
              data: { "app--budget--rule-form-target": "item" } %>
          <p class="form-hint">
            Leave this on the whole category for a rate that covers all of its spending, or name one
            item for a bill of its own.
          </p>
          <% if rule_form.errors[:item_id].any? %>
            <p class="form-error"><%= rule_form.errors[:item_id].to_sentence %></p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%# ── 3. TYPE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            BILL, USAGE OR CHOICE (§3, Henry's ruling of 2026-09-04) — "bills are must pays, usage
            is like power bill (can be lowered by adjusting life), choice is like a restaurant".
            It is a fact about the RULE and not about the category, because one category can carry a
            fixed bill on one of its items and a discretionary catch-all beside it, and it decides
            the GIVE-WAY ORDER when free money goes below zero: choice gives way first, bill last.

            REQUIRED, WITH NO RADIO PRESELECTED ON A HAND-MADE RULE. The column defaults to `usage`
            so an untyped row in the database claims neither "must be paid" nor "up to you", but a
            form that pre-answered the question would let a user write a rule without ever making
            the choice the give-way order is built on. A proposal DOES arrive typed, and its radio is
            checked (§3: dated bills propose `bill`, rates and drifts propose `usage`). %>
        <div>
          <span class="form-label">Type</span>
          <% type_help = { "bill" => "must be paid", "usage" => "a need you can trim", "choice" => "up to you" } %>
          <div class="mt-2 space-y-2">
            <% Budget.rule_types.keys.each do |type| %>
              <label class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded border border-gray-200 p-3 cursor-pointer hover:border-gray-300">
                <%= f.radio_button :rule_type, type, class: "form-radio mt-1" %>
                <span class="flex-1">
                  <span class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900"><%= type.capitalize %></span>
                  <span class="block text-xs text-gray-500"><%= type_help[type] %></span>
                </span>
              </label>
            <% end %>
          </div>
          <% if rule_form.errors[:rule_type].any? %>
            <p class="form-error">Type <%= rule_form.errors[:rule_type].to_sentence %></p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%# ── 4. AMOUNT ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            ITS UNIT IS NAMED BY THE SCHEDULE BELOW, and `#budget_amount_hint` says so in the rule's
            own words — "per period", "a month", "every 6 months", "once".

            ** ONE ROW'S UNIT IS THE RECORD'S AND NOT THE SCHEDULE'S (fix round 1 — MED-5). ** A
            `monthly`-no-anchor rule is read back as "Every period" (§5's ruling), so the record this
            hint would otherwise read has already been re-shaped and would call a month's figure a
            period's. `RuleForm#amount_unit` is the row's own unit, and the note under the field says
            what saving will do to it. %>
        <div>
          <%= f.input :amount,
              as: :decimal,
              label: "Rule Amount",
              input_html: {
                placeholder: "0.00",
                step: 0.01,
                min: 0
              },
              hint: budget_amount_hint(budget, unit: rule_form.amount_unit) %>

          <% if note = budget_monthly_conversion_note(rule_form) %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-warning" data-monthly-conversion><%= note %></p>
          <% end %>

          <%# WHAT THE RULE SAYS TODAY, beside what a drift suggestion is proposing it should say.
              The prefilled figure is in THE RULE'S OWN UNIT — SuggestionEngine inverts
              `Budget#steady_ask` before putting it on the wire, precisely so that nothing here
              converts it — and the label above already names that unit, so the two figures on
              screen are directly comparable. Shown only when they differ: on every other edit the
              field IS the current amount and repeating it would be one number printed twice. %>
          <% if @current_amount.present? && rule_form.amount.present? && rule_form.amount.to_d != @current_amount.to_d %>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500" data-current-amount>
              <%# ** THE UNIT IS `RuleForm#amount_unit`, THE SAME ONE THE LABEL ABOVE READS. ** It was
                  `budget_rule_basis_phrase(budget)`, and `budget` is the record AFTER the words have
                  been applied — so on the one row §5's read-back re-shapes (a `monthly`-no-anchor
                  rule, which reads back as "Every period"), the hint said "asks for a month" and
                  this line said "per period" about the same figure, an inch apart. Two clauses about
                  one unit must not be able to disagree. %>
              Currently <%= number_to_currency(@current_amount) %>
              <%= rule_form.amount_unit || budget_rule_basis_phrase(budget) %>.
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <%# ── 5. WHEN IS IT NEEDED? ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            THE TWO SHAPES OF §2, AS TWO WORDS (two-shapes spec §5, Henry's ruling of 2026-09-05).
            "Every period" is a spending allowance that resets with the paycheck; "By a date" is
            money saved up toward a day — a bill or a goal, which are ONE shape — and it reveals the
            date and a "repeats every N months" checkbox. `RuleForm` maps the pair onto the three
            columns, and the errors from `Budget`'s own shape validations land back here (under the
            control that chose them) rather than under a `basis` field this form does not have.

            ** "Every month" LEFT THE RADIOS AND NOT THE MODEL (§5's ruling). ** `monthly` with no
            due date is a legal row and `SuggestionEngine` still writes one; it is not a shape people
            write by hand, so the form does not offer it and `RuleForm.from` reads such a row back as
            "Every period" with its amount shown per month. Saving it unchanged converts it, which is
            the ruling taken rather than a defect hidden.

            ** THE UNSPENT-MONEY STEP AND ITS TARGET FIELD ARE DELETED (§7). ** They asked what
            becomes of money the period did not spend and revealed a Target under "Builds up". A fund
            IS a dated rule whose amount is its target, so "builds up toward $5,000" is written as
            "$5,000" in step 4 and "by Jun 1, 2027" here — and `budgets.carries-over` and
            `budgets.target-amount` are dropped.

            TASK 4 REBUILDS THIS PAGE on §5's mocks (a two-column layout with a sticky preview and
            suggestion chips). What is here is the same controls in the new words, working. %>
        <div>
          <span class="form-label">When is it needed?</span>
          <% schedules = [
               ["per_period", "Every period", "a spending allowance that resets with your paycheck"],
               ["by_date", "By a date", "money saved up toward a day — a bill or a goal"]
             ] %>
          <div class="mt-2 space-y-2">
            <% schedules.each do |value, label, help| %>
              <label class="flex items-start gap-3 rounded border border-gray-200 p-3 cursor-pointer hover:border-gray-300">
                <% radio_data = { action: "change->app--budget--rule-form#refresh" } %>
                <%= f.radio_button :schedule, value, class: "form-radio mt-1", data: radio_data %>
                <span class="flex-1">
                  <span class="block text-sm font-medium text-gray-900"><%= label %></span>
                  <span class="block text-xs text-gray-500"><%= help %></span>
                </span>
              </label>
            <% end %>
          </div>
          <% if rule_form.errors[:schedule].any? %>
            <p class="form-error">When it is needed <%= rule_form.errors[:schedule].to_sentence %></p>
          <% end %>

          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 pl-3 border-l-2 border-gray-100">
            <%# THE DATE. It rode HIDDEN beside the basis until Henry's real-use testing of
                2026-08-20: `SuggestionEngine#next_due_on` walks the LAST PAYMENT forward by the
                interval, so a bill paid late once proposed a rule whose every future due date was
                late with it — and the amount on the same form was editable while the date that
                decides when the money is needed was not. %>
            <div data-rule-reveal
                 data-app--budget--rule-form-target="anchorField"
                 <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" %>>
              <%= f.input :anchor_date,
                  as: :date,
                  html5: true,
                  label: "Due",
                  hint: "The day the money has to be there. Change it if the last payment was early or late." %>
            </div>

            <%# REPEATS — a checkbox rather than a third schedule, because "every 6 months" is a
                detail OF "by a date" and not a different answer to when the money is needed. Its own
                reveal is the interval below; two nested reveals would each stash and clear the
                other's inputs on the way past, so every reveal on this form is a leaf. %>
            <div data-rule-reveal
                 data-app--budget--rule-form-target="repeatsField"
                 <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" %>>
              <%= f.input :repeats,
                  as: :boolean,
                  label: "Repeats every N months",
                  input_html: { data: { action: "change->app--budget--rule-form#refresh" } },
                  hint: "leave this off for a one-off bill or a savings goal" %>
            </div>

            <%# THE INTERVAL, revealed by the checkbox alone. §2's row 4 is
                `monthly + interval + anchor`; a form offering the N without the date could submit
                row 2 with an N > 1, which `shape_must_be_valid` refuses. %>
            <div data-rule-reveal
                 data-app--budget--rule-form-target="intervalField"
                 <%= "hidden" unless rule_form.schedule == "by_date" && rule_form.repeats? %>>
              <%= f.input :interval_months,
                  as: :integer,
                  label: "Comes round every (months)",
                  input_html: { min: 1, step: 1 },
                  hint: "6 for a half-yearly bill, 3 for a quarterly one." %>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Action Buttons %>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-end gap-3">
      <%# Back where the rule is listed, which is the Budget page — the same destination
          BudgetsController sends a save to. %>
      <%= link_to "Cancel", budget_page_path, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>

      <%= f.submit rule_form.persisted? ? "Update rule" : "Create rule",
          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
